  "text_input": "A 63-year-old female presented with bilateral lower limb swelling for 15 days, initially involving the feet and extending to the knees, accompanied by facial puffiness. She reported nausea for 5 days and two episodes of vomiting 3 days prior. She is a known case of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus (16 years) and Systemic Hypertension (8 years). Physical examination revealed pallor and bilateral lower limb edema. Investigations showed an eGFR of 25 ml/min/1.73m², Grade 3 hypertensive retinopathy, and bilateral proliferative diabetic retinopathy. Iron profile indicated iron deficiency anemia (Iron 50.7, Ferritin 69.2, TSAT 18%). 2D Echo showed concentric LVH, mild AR, and an EF of 58%. She was treated with insulin (Mixtard, Actrapid), diuretics (Lasix), antihypertensives (Amlong, Cilacar), and iron supplementation (FCM).",
